WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Linear regression.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.800
It is simple, readable, and the baseline every fancier model has to beat.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.800
Linear regression fits a line, or in many dimensions a hyperplane, through your data.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.300
The prediction is a weight times the input, plus a bias.

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.495
It picks the line that minimizes the squared error between predictions and targets.

6
00:00:21.495 --> 00:00:23.500
And its coefficients are readable.

7
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.000
Picture temperature against stellar flux.

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.667
Each dot is a planet.

9
00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:31.472
The fitted line passes through the cloud.

10
00:00:31.472 --> 00:00:35.200
The vertical gaps between dots and line are called residuals.

11
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.340
Square them and add them up, and you have the loss.

12
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.233
The best line makes that smallest.

13
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:44.567
Here is the surprise: no gradient descent is needed.

14
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:48.967
The best weights come from a closed form solution, called the normal equation.

15
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.200
One piece is the inverse of X transpose X.

16
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.185
The other is X transpose times the targets.

17
00:00:55.185 --> 00:00:57.433
Solve it directly, and you are done.

18
00:00:58.633 --> 00:00:59.967
Here it is both ways.

19
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:04.667
We add a column of ones for the bias, and solve the normal equation directly.

20
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:12.267
Scikit-learn's linear regression lands on the same numbers: about one eighty for the intercept, and ninety five for the slope.

21
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:16.200
Before getting fancy, fit the simple baseline.

22
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.700
Regress temperature on stellar flux and orbital period.

23
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.433
The flux coefficient is about ninety five kelvin per Earth flux, a number an astronomer can sanity check against physics.

24
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:34.100
Later, when XGBoost beats it by eight percent, you know exactly what the extra complexity bought.

25
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.033
Let's recap.

26
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.600
Linear regression minimizes squared error.

27
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.600
Its coefficients are readable.

28
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.367
It has a closed form, the normal equation.

29
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.660
And it is the baseline to beat.

30
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.002
If a plain line ties your deep model, ship the line.

31
00:01:49.002 --> 00:01:50.867
Next up: logistic regression.
